WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.020 --> 00:00:05.292
In science fiction,

2
00:00:05.292 --> 00:00:10.516
a wormhole is a tunnel through space. It joins two far places by a short path.

3
00:00:10.953 --> 00:00:13.961
Einstein's theory of gravity allows such shapes on

4
00:00:13.961 --> 00:00:17.630
paper. The narrowest part of the tunnel is called the throat.

5
00:00:18.053 --> 00:00:22.418
But a throat does not stay open by itself. Something must hold it open.

6
00:00:22.853 --> 00:00:26.653
A 2026 research paper asks one narrow question.

7
00:00:27.087 --> 00:00:30.567
Can the quantum vacuum alone hold a wormhole throat open?

8
00:00:32.087 --> 00:00:36.640
In 1988, Morris and Thorne showed what a throat needs. At the throat,

9
00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:41.061
the source must have a property that ordinary matter does not have.

10
00:00:41.487 --> 00:00:42.738
In technical words,

11
00:00:42.738 --> 00:00:45.307
the source must violate the null energy

12
00:00:45.307 --> 00:00:48.469
condition. Physicists call such a source exotic.

13
00:00:48.887 --> 00:00:52.912
Quantum physics gives a candidate. Empty space, the vacuum,

14
00:00:52.912 --> 00:00:55.982
is not completely empty. In curved spacetime,

15
00:00:55.982 --> 00:00:58.643
it carries a small energy and pressure.

16
00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:00.110
In some places,

17
00:01:00.110 --> 00:01:03.280
this vacuum stress can have the unusual sign. So

18
00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:06.648
it is the standard candidate to hold a throat open.

19
00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:11.149
In the semiclassical approach, the shape of spacetime is classical,

20
00:01:11.149 --> 00:01:15.117
and the vacuum stress is its source, through Einstein's equations.

21
00:01:16.653 --> 00:01:20.821
The paper does not try to build a wormhole. It takes a fixed throat shape,

22
00:01:20.821 --> 00:01:22.173
and it tests that shape.

23
00:01:22.620 --> 00:01:24.792
It uses one kind of quantum field:

24
00:01:24.792 --> 00:01:29.265
a massless scalar field with conformal coupling, in the static vacuum.

25
00:01:29.687 --> 00:01:30.588
On each shape,

26
00:01:30.588 --> 00:01:33.226
it computes the full vacuum stress at the

27
00:01:33.226 --> 00:01:36.122
throat. This is a long numerical calculation.

28
00:01:36.553 --> 00:01:38.211
Then it asks: at the throat,

29
00:01:38.211 --> 00:01:42.888
can Einstein's equations hold? There are three of them, one for each direction.

30
00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:46.939
One is for the energy, one is for the pull along the tunnel,

31
00:01:46.939 --> 00:01:49.352
and one is for the pressure around it. A

32
00:01:49.352 --> 00:01:52.067
real throat needs all three at the same time.

33
00:01:52.487 --> 00:01:57.621
The paper allows N identical copies of the field. More copies give more stress.

34
00:01:58.053 --> 00:02:01.218
The calculation has one free length,

35
00:02:01.218 --> 00:02:07.195
ℓ. The paper sets it to the Planck length, about 1.6 × 10⁻³⁵ metres.

36
00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:12.782
It looks for throats with a radius from 1 to 1,000 Planck lengths. In all,

37
00:02:12.782 --> 00:02:14.735
it examines 42 fixed shapes.

38
00:02:16.253 --> 00:02:18.272
First, on three smooth throats,

39
00:02:18.272 --> 00:02:22.699
the paper compares an older approximation with the full calculation.

40
00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:28.106
The approximation keeps only a local part of the stress. On all three throats,

41
00:02:28.106 --> 00:02:32.644
this part has the sign that a throat needs: a tension along the tunnel.

42
00:02:33.087 --> 00:02:37.144
But the full calculation gives the opposite sign. The part that

43
00:02:37.144 --> 00:02:41.653
the approximation leaves out carries at least 94 percent of the total.

44
00:02:42.087 --> 00:02:45.182
So on these throats, the full stress is a pressure,

45
00:02:45.182 --> 00:02:48.094
not the tension that a throat needs. The sign of

46
00:02:48.094 --> 00:02:51.493
the approximation does not decide the sign of the total.

47
00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:54.064
The paper does not test the earlier

48
00:02:54.064 --> 00:02:57.434
self-consistent solutions that used this approximation.

49
00:02:58.953 --> 00:03:02.426
Next, the size of the throat. When the size changes,

50
00:03:02.426 --> 00:03:07.235
the vacuum stress changes through a logarithm. One number at the throat,

51
00:03:07.235 --> 00:03:08.637
B, sets how strongly.

52
00:03:09.087 --> 00:03:11.556
The paper finds a short formula for B. It

53
00:03:11.556 --> 00:03:14.206
depends on only two properties of the shape.

54
00:03:14.653 --> 00:03:17.786
One is how fast the tunnel opens out at the throat.

55
00:03:17.786 --> 00:03:21.165
The other is how fast the rate of clocks changes there.

56
00:03:21.587 --> 00:03:24.014
In the plane of these two properties,

57
00:03:24.014 --> 00:03:27.163
two curves divide the cases. Between the curves,

58
00:03:27.163 --> 00:03:29.985
B is positive. Outside them, B is negative.

59
00:03:30.420 --> 00:03:31.550
If B is positive,

60
00:03:31.550 --> 00:03:37.067
the pull along the tunnel allows only throats smaller than a cap. If B is negative,

61
00:03:37.067 --> 00:03:39.925
it allows only throats larger than a floor.

62
00:03:41.453 --> 00:03:46.072
On all 21 shapes with positive B, the cap lies below the Planck length.

63
00:03:46.520 --> 00:03:51.650
With one copy of the field, on the 15 shapes of the main family,

64
00:03:51.650 --> 00:03:55.898
the pull equation is solved only for throats of about

65
00:03:55.898 --> 00:04:00.867
0.01 to 0.05 Planck lengths. More copies do not raise the cap.

66
00:04:01.287 --> 00:04:02.822
Below the Planck length,

67
00:04:02.822 --> 00:04:05.574
this kind of calculation is not expected to

68
00:04:05.574 --> 00:04:09.094
apply. The paper gives these sizes no physical meaning.

69
00:04:09.520 --> 00:04:14.837
In that family, the energy equation changes sign near only one point,

70
00:04:14.837 --> 00:04:19.615
at about 0.05 Planck lengths. There, the third equation fails.

71
00:04:21.153 --> 00:04:22.369
On the other side,

72
00:04:22.369 --> 00:04:27.097
B is negative. The paper samples 13 shapes on one face of this region,

73
00:04:27.097 --> 00:04:30.473
chosen so that the energy equation becomes simple.

74
00:04:30.920 --> 00:04:35.286
At 12 of these shapes, the sign of the stress is certain. There,

75
00:04:35.286 --> 00:04:39.311
the pull equation does have solutions over the whole range,

76
00:04:39.311 --> 00:04:43.064
from 1 to 1,000 Planck lengths. Depending on the shape,

77
00:04:43.064 --> 00:04:46.884
it needs at least 59 to about 1,400 copies of the field.

78
00:04:47.320 --> 00:04:51.804
But on this face, the error of the energy equation is one number, C,

79
00:04:51.804 --> 00:04:56.419
divided by the radial stress. So its sign does not depend on the size.

80
00:04:56.853 --> 00:04:59.936
The computed C is negative at all 13 points,

81
00:04:59.936 --> 00:05:04.140
beyond its uncertainty. So wherever the pull equation holds,

82
00:05:04.140 --> 00:05:05.961
the energy equation fails.

83
00:05:06.387 --> 00:05:10.890
The third equation crosses zero at only three points. But there, too,

84
00:05:10.890 --> 00:05:15.459
the energy equation fails. So the three equations never hold together.

85
00:05:15.887 --> 00:05:20.771
Version 1.1 adds three shapes on the same face with a positive mass,

86
00:05:20.771 --> 00:05:25.010
as seen from far away. The energy equation fails there too.

87
00:05:26.553 --> 00:05:28.346
So, for the shapes examined:

88
00:05:28.346 --> 00:05:31.932
the static vacuum of this one field does not, by itself,

89
00:05:31.932 --> 00:05:35.582
give a throat in the range where the calculation applies.

90
00:05:36.020 --> 00:05:38.859
Changing the free length changes the sizes,

91
00:05:38.859 --> 00:05:41.764
but not the answers. A scan of a second free

92
00:05:41.764 --> 00:05:45.726
constant gives no candidate on the negative-B shapes either.

93
00:05:46.153 --> 00:05:50.151
This points in the same direction as earlier general arguments,

94
00:05:50.151 --> 00:05:53.640
which suggest that throats held open by quantum effects

95
00:05:53.640 --> 00:05:57.764
stay near the Planck scale. The paper makes no claim of priority.

96
00:05:58.187 --> 00:06:02.811
The paper does not say that wormholes are impossible. It tests one field,

97
00:06:02.811 --> 00:06:05.979
in one state, on fixed shapes, at the throat only.

98
00:06:06.420 --> 00:06:10.988
Other fields, other states, long throats, the full self-consistent shape,

99
00:06:10.988 --> 00:06:16.056
and stability are not examined. And the grids of shapes are samples, not a proof.

100
00:06:16.487 --> 00:06:21.590
Many numbers also carry a reservation on a conservation check near the

101
00:06:21.590 --> 00:06:26.767
throat. A recalculation of 24 shapes at finer settings kept every sign.

102
00:06:28.287 --> 00:06:33.058
The work had three layers. Each layer closed only after an independent review.

103
00:06:33.487 --> 00:06:37.896
The reviewers were AI sessions that shared no context with the writer.

104
00:06:37.896 --> 00:06:42.369
Every number in the paper is checked by machine against frozen records.

105
00:06:42.787 --> 00:06:44.046
The computations and

106
00:06:44.046 --> 00:06:48.644
the text were prepared with AI assistants. The author chose the question,

107
00:06:48.644 --> 00:06:53.368
made every decision on direction, and takes responsibility for the content.

108
00:06:53.787 --> 00:06:58.589
The author has not reproduced the computations. The author has read the title,

109
00:06:58.589 --> 00:07:01.114
the abstract and the statements of scope.

110
00:07:01.553 --> 00:07:06.178
The paper, the scripts and the records are on Zenodo. The link is below the video.
